al solution fails, it must be discarded.\n\nTherefore, no valid approach is present in the list.\n</reasoning>\n<results>\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nThe partial solution provided is i0: "84 + 80 - 37 + 27 / 3 * 5".\n\nWe must evaluate whether this expression correctly uses all the given numbers: 37, 27, 3, 84, 5, 80 — each at most once. The expression includes all six numbers, so that condition is satisfied.\n\nNow, we analyze the arithmetic evaluation of this expression using standard order of operations (PEMDAS):\n\n- Division and multiplication are performed left to right before addition and subtraction.\n\nBreakdown:\n- 27 / 3 = 9\n- 9 * 5 = 45\n- Then: 84 + 80 = 164\n- 164 - 37 = 127\n- 127 + 45 = 172\n\nResult: 172, which is not 152.\n\nTherefore, this expression does not yield the correct target value of 152. It fails the correctness requirement.\n\nSince there is only one partial solution provided (i0), and it is incorrect (it evaluates to 172, not 152), there are no valid approaches among the given partial solutions.\n\nThus, despite being syntactically correct and using all numbers, the solution does not compute 152 and is therefore invalid.\n\nNo valid or correct approach is present in the given partial solutions.\n\n</reasoning>\n<results>\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nThe partial solution provided is i0: (81 - 45) * (27 + 48) - 64 + 20.\n\nWe evaluate the expression step by step to verify whether it equals 719:\n\n1. (81 - 45) = 36  \n2. (27 + 48) = 75  \n3. 36 * 75 = 2700  \n4. 2700 - 64 = 2636  \n5. 2636 + 20 = 2656  \n\nThe result is 2656, which is not 719. Therefore, this expression does not yield the target value.\n\nSince the problem requires the expression to evaluate to 719 using the given numbers exactly once (or at most once), and this expression fails to do so, it is invalid.\n\nThere are no other partial solutions listed to compare against. Therefore, among the provided partial solutions (only i0 is present), there are no valid approaches.\n\