osed. Hence, it is not valid.\n\nFinal conclusion: There is no valid approach among the partial solutions. But since only one is given and it is incorrect, and no others exist, the set contains no valid solution.\n\nYet, the task says "identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if none exist, then no selection.\n\nBut the output format requires indices. Given that the only partial solution is i0 and it is invalid, and no valid one is present, we cannot select any.\n\nHowever, perhaps the original problem allows for using numbers in a way that adds zero, such as (26 - 14) = 12, and then subtract 12? But 462 + 6 = 468, and 468 - 12 = 456 — not 468.\n\nNo way to add zero with 26 and 14.\n\nThus, the expression cannot use all numbers and reach 468.\n\nTherefore, no valid approach exists.\n\nBut since the instruction says to select one, and only i0 is present, and it is the only one that comes close, and the rest are just attempts, perhaps it is the only one that shows a logical path.\n\nBut it is still invalid due to missing numbers.\n\nTherefore, after careful reasoning, there is no valid approach in the provided partial solutions.\n\nBut the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — and since only one exists, and it is flawed, we must reject it.\n\nThus, no valid solution.\n\nBut to comply with the output format, and since no valid one exists, and only one is listed, and it is incorrect, we cannot output any.\n\nHowever, the problem may have a typo or the solution may exist via a different path.\n\nLet’s test if any combination can reach 468:\n\nTry: (14 + 26) = 40  \n49 * 6 = 294  \n294 + 40 = 334  \n468 - 334 = 134 → 17 * 7 = 119 → 134 - 119 = 15 → not matching.\n\nTry: 17 * 6 = 102  \n49 * 7 = 343  \n343 + 102 = 445  \n468 - 445 = 23 → 26 - 14 = 12 → no.\n\nTry: 26 * 6 = 156  \n156 + 49 = 205  \n205 + 17 = 222  \n222 + 14 = 236  \n236 + 7 = 243 → too small.\n\nTry: 49 * 14 = 686 → too big.\n\nTry: (26 + 14) = 40  \n(49 + 17) = 66  \n66 * 7 = 