n\nBut the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — and since only one exists, and it is flawed, we must reject it.\n\nThus, no valid solution.\n\nBut to satisfy the format, and given that the problem may have been misstated, and the intended solution is (49+17)*7 + 6 = 468, and the numbers 26 and 14 are unused, perhaps they are not required? But the problem says "using all the numbers".\n\nTherefore, it is impossible.\n\nThus, the partial solution i0 is incorrect.\n\nNo valid approach exists.\n\nBut since no other solutions are provided, and only i0 is present, and it is incorrect, we output nothing.\n\nHowever, the output format requires indices.\n\nGiven the constraints, and that no valid approach exists, but the only one that shows a correct mathematical path is i0 (even if incomplete), and the others are just random tries, we select i0 as the only one with a structured, logical approach — despite its flaw in number usage.\n\nBut strictly speaking, it fails to use all numbers.\n\nTherefore, it is invalid.\n\nAfter full evaluation, there is no valid approach.\n\nBut to fulfill the output requirement, and since no other indices exist, and i0 is the only one that comes close, we select it as the only candidate.\n\nFinal decision: Despite the flaw, i0 contains the only plausible logical path and is the only one that attempts a correct structure. The others are just random trials.\n\nThus, it is the most diverse and only structured approach, even if not fully valid.\n\nTherefore, we select i0.\n\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nThe partial solution provided is i0: (74 + 56) * 9 - (59 + 71 - 44) = 492.\n\nFirst, evaluate the expression step by step to verify correctness:\n- (74 + 56) = 130\n- 130 * 9 = 1170\n- (59 + 71) = 130\n- (130 - 44) = 86\n- 1170 - 86 = 1084\n\n1084 ≠ 492 → The expression does not evaluate to 492.\n\nTherefore, the given partial solution is mathematically incorrect.\n\nSince only one partial solutio