t.\n\nHowever, the prompt says: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches" — implying that at least one might be valid.\n\nBut none are.\n\nTherefore, the correct conclusion is that no valid approach exists in the given partial solutions.\n\nBut since we are required to output something, and the only possible interpretation is that one of them might contain a valid path (even if not fully realized), and given that i0 includes a slightly more varied exploration (e.g., (28 - 18) = 10, (43 - 23) = 20, sum = 30), which is a minor insight, it shows a more diverse thinking pattern — using differences to generate known values.\n\nStill, no expression is formed.\n\nThus, despite the diversity in thought, no valid solution is constructed.\n\nFinal decision: both are incorrect. But since the task requires selecting one, and i0 contains a slightly more diverse exploration (e.g., using differences to form 30), it is the more distinct in approach, even if still invalid.\n\nTherefore, we select i0 as the most diverse among incorrect approaches — but it is still not valid.\n\nHowever, the instruction says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if no valid approach exists, then the task is impossible.\n\nBut the problem states: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if there are none, then the output should be empty.\n\nBut the example output includes indices.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, and the requirement to provide an output, and since both are incorrect and redundant, and no valid expression is present, we must conclude that no valid approach exists.\n\nBut the instruction says: "Select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — so even if invalid, we must pick one.\n\nThus, between i0 and i1, i0 is more diverse in its exploration (e.g., difference-based grouping), so it is selected.\n\nTherefore, the only reasonable output is i0, as the most diverse approach, even though it is not valid.\n\nBut the task is 