olutions that results in 936.  \nTherefore, both i0 and i1 are invalid due to incorrect evaluation.\n\nSince no solution is valid, there are no correct approaches to select. However, the instruction says to "identify the unique, valid approaches" and "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — implying at least one valid one exists.\n\nBut upon rechecking:  \nIs there a possibility of a miscalculation?\n\nRecheck i0:  \n36 * 17 = 612  \n612 + 59 = 671  \n671 + 21 = 692  \n692 + 12 = 704  \n704 + 17 = 721 → Still 721, not 936.\n\ni1:  \n612 + 252 = 864  \n864 + 59 = 923  \n923 - 17 = 906 → Still not 936.\n\nNo combination of these operations reaches 936.  \nThus, both are invalid.\n\nBut the task is to filter out incorrect approaches and identify unique valid ones. Since there are no valid approaches, and both are incorrect, they are both to be discarded.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches among the given partial solutions.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — implying at least one valid one exists. But based on arithmetic, neither works.\n\nPossibility: Did we misread the numbers?  \nTarget is 936.  \nTry to see if 36 * 26 = 936 → 936 ÷ 36 = 26 → so 36 * 26 = 936.  \nCan we make 26 from the other numbers: 21, 59, 12, 17, 17?\n\n21 + 12 = 33 → too big  \n17 + 17 = 34  \n59 - 21 = 38  \nNo obvious way to make 26.\n\nTry 936 ÷ 12 = 78 → 12 * 78 = 936  \nCan we make 78 from others? 36, 21, 59, 17, 17  \n36 + 21 = 57 → 57 + 17 = 74 → 74 + 17 = 91 → too big  \n59 + 17 = 76 → +2 = 78 → need 2 → not available  \n59 + 17 = 76 → +2 → no\n\nTry 936 ÷ 17 ≈ 55.06 → not helpful  \nTry 936 ÷ 59 ≈ 15.87 → not helpful\n\nNo clear path to 936.\n\nThus, both i0 and i1 are mathematically incorrect.\n\nSince no valid solution exists in the provided partials, and both are incorrect, they are both invalid.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches to select.\n\nBut the task says to "identify the unique, valid approaches" — if none exis