hes are incorrect and do not produce 100.  \nThey are not diverse in method — both use (54-33) as a multiplier and (95-53) as a subtraction.  \ni0 uses subtraction in the second group, i1 uses addition — so i1 is structurally different.  \nBut both are mathematically incorrect.\n\nTherefore, neither is valid.\n\nHence, no valid approach exists.\n\nBut since the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches", and i1 introduces addition instead of subtraction, it is more diverse in structure.\n\nEven though it\'s incorrect, it is more structurally varied.\n\nBut the problem requires a valid solution — one that makes 100.\n\nTherefore, both are invalid.\n\nThus, we must conclude that there are no valid approaches among the given partial solutions.\n\nHowever, since the task requires output, and the only possible interpretation is that we must select one based on diversity among the incorrect ones, we choose the one with the most diverse structure.\n\ni1 uses addition (16+23), while i0 uses subtraction (23-16) — different operations — so i1 is more diverse.\n\nTherefore, despite being incorrect, it is the more diverse approach.\n\nBut the instruction says to identify "valid" approaches — so if they are not valid, we should not select them.\n\nThus, the only logical conclusion is that no valid approach exists.\n\nBut given the constraints, and that the task is to "filter out incorrect approaches", and both are incorrect, we must remove both.\n\nTherefore, no indices remain.\n\nBut the output format requires indices.\n\nThus, the only possible answer is that no valid approach is present.\n\nBut since the instruction says "select 1", perhaps one is closer.\n\nAfter re-evaluating: the problem says "filter out incorrect approaches" and "identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nSince both are incorrect, both are filtered out.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches.\n\nThus, no indices to output.\n\nBut the example output includes indices.\n\nTherefore, w