se.\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\ni1\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nWe are to evaluate which of the given partial solutions correctly and uniquely form 358 using all of the numbers: 70, 74, 67, 86, 80, 19, each at most once.\n\nFirst, analyze each solution:\n\ni0: The expression is (86 + 80 + 74 + 70 + 67) - 19.  \nStep-by-step:  \n86 + 80 = 166  \n166 + 74 = 240  \n240 + 70 = 310  \n310 + 67 = 377  \n377 - 19 = 358 ✅  \nAll numbers used exactly once. The logic is additive with a single subtraction. This is valid and correct.\n\ni1: The expression is (86 - 74) * (80 - 67) + 70 + 19  \nCompute:  \n86 - 74 = 12  \n80 - 67 = 13  \n12 * 13 = 156  \n156 + 70 = 226  \n226 + 19 = 245 ≠ 358 ❌  \nThis does not equal 358. It uses only 4 numbers (86,74,80,67,70,19) — all are used, but the result is incorrect. So this is invalid.\n\ni2: The expression is (70 + 86) * 3 + 67 - 80 + 19 - 74  \nThis uses the number 3, which is not in the original list of numbers (70,74,67,86,80,19). The problem states to use only the given numbers. Introducing a constant like 3 is not allowed. This is invalid.\n\ni3: Identical to i1: (86 - 74) * (80 - 67) + 70 + 19  \nSame incorrect computation as i1. It is a duplicate of i1 and also fails to produce 358. Invalid.\n\nThus, only i0 produces the correct result using only the given numbers without introducing external constants or incorrect operations.\n\nAlthough i1 and i3 are identical in expression, they are duplicates and represent the same invalid approach.\n\ni2 introduces an invalid number (3), which violates the constraint of using only the provided numbers.\n\nTherefore, only one valid approach exists: i0.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to select "2.0 of the most diverse approaches" — implying two distinct valid approaches. But in this case, only one valid and correct solution exists.\n\nGiven that, we must consider whether any other solution, despite being incorrect, could be considered a "valid approach" in a different logical structure. But