  \n36 * 107 = 3852 → way off.\n\nNo expression yields 491.\n\nTherefore, none of the partial solutions are valid.\n\nBut the instruction says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" and "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches".\n\nGiven that all expressions are mathematically incorrect (they do not evaluate to 491), and all use the numbers but with wrong results, none are valid.\n\nHowever, if the problem is to *filter out incorrect approaches*, and only keep those that are valid (i.e., correct in result), then since none are valid, there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches" — implying at least two exist.\n\nPossibility: a typo in the problem or in the expressions.\n\nBut based strictly on evaluation, all expressions are incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the only logical conclusion is that none are valid.\n\nBut the task says: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nSince there are no valid approaches, we must conclude that the set is empty.\n\nHowever, the output format requires selecting two indices.\n\nThis contradiction suggests a deeper issue.\n\nWait — perhaps one of the expressions was intended to be correct, and we made a calculation error.\n\nRecheck i1: (91 - 55) * 10 + 97 + 78 - 39  \n= 36 * 10 = 360  \n360 + 97 = 457  \n457 + 78 = 535  \n535 - 39 = 496 → not 491.\n\nDifference of 5.\n\nWhat if it were (91 - 55) * 10 + 97 + 78 - 40? But 40 not in list.\n\nNo.\n\nWhat about (97 - 78) = 19; 19 * 10 = 190; 190 + 91 = 281; 281 + 39 = 320; 320 + 55 = 375; no.\n\nNo expression gives 491.\n\nThus, all are incorrect.\n\nBut the instruction says to "identify the unique, valid approaches" — if none are valid, then no selection.\n\nBut the output requires two indices.\n\nPossibility: the problem may have a typo, or the expressions are meant to be evaluated differently.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps "valid" refers to structural validity — i.e., using the numbers, correct operations, no duplicates — not necessarily yielding the correct result.\n\nBut the problem says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" from a set of partial solutions — and the context is to calculate 491.\n\nThus, "valid" must mean "correctly evaluates to 491".\n\nSince none do, there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut the instruction says to select two.\n\nThis suggests a misinterpretation.\n\nRe-reading the task: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches"\n\nSo we must identify which ones are valid (i.e., correct), and select two of the most diverse.\n\nBut if none are valid, then we cannot select any.\n\nBut perhaps one of them is close and a typo?\n\nWait — i0 and i2 are identical — duplicates.\n\ni1 and i3 are different in structure.\n\nBut both fail.\n\nThus, no valid approaches.\n\nBut the problem says "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches" — implying at least two exist.\n\nGiven that all expressions are mathematically incorrect, and no one evaluates to 491, all are incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the only valid action is to filter out all — but we are to select two.\n\nThis contradiction implies that either:\n- The expressions were meant to be correct, or\n- We are to select based on structural diversity, even if incorrect.\n\nBut the instruction says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" — not just diverse.\n\nThus, if no approach is valid (i.e., correct), then no selection.\n\nBut since the output format requires two indices, and the problem may have a typo, we must assume that one or more expressions are correct — but our calculations show otherwise.\n\nDouble-check i1: (91 - 55) * 10 + 97 + 78 - 39  \n= 36 * 10 = 360  \n360 + 97 = 457  \n457 + 78 = 535  \n535 - 39 = 496 → 5 too high.\n\nWhat if it were minus 46? Not available.\n\nNo.\n\nWhat if: (97 - 78) * 10 = 190  \n190 + 91 = 281  \n281 + 39 = 320  \n320 + 55 = 375 → no.\n\n(91 + 39) = 130  \n(97 + 78) = 175  \n175 + 130 = 305  \n305 + 55 = 360  \n360 + 10 = 370 → no.\n\nNo combination seems to reach 491.\n\nThus, all expressions are incorrect.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut the instruction says to select two.\n\nGiven the contradiction, and the fact that the problem might have a typo, and that the task is to filter out incorrect ones, we must conclude that all are incorrect.\n\nBut since the output requires two indices, and the only structural diversity is between i0/i2 (same) and i1/i3 (different), perhaps the intended valid ones are i1 and i3 — even if wrong.\n\nBut that contradicts "valid".\n\nAlternatively, perhaps "valid" means "uses the numbers and operations correctly" — i.e., no invalid operations, uses only allowed numbers, each at most once.\n\nAll do that.\n\nSo perhaps "valid" means structurally sound and uses all numbers.\n\nAll expressions use all six numbers: 55, 39, 91, 10, 97, 78.\n\ni0: uses 97,78,55,10,91,39 → all\ni1: uses 91,55,10,97,78,39 → all\ni2: same as i0 → all\ni3: uses 91,55,10,97,39,78 → all\n\nSo all use all numbers.\n\nAll use basic operations.\n\nSo all are structurally valid.\n\nBut only if "valid" means "structurally sound", not "correct result".\n\nBut the problem says "calculate 491", so correctness is required.\n\nTherefore, only if the expression evaluates to 491 is it valid.\n\nSince none do, no valid approach.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches".\n\nGiven that, and the only structural differences:\n\n- i0 and i2 are identical → not diverse\n- i1 and i3 are different in structure:\n  - i1: (91 - 55) * 10 + 97 + 78 - 39\n  - i3: (91 - 55) * (10 + 97) - 39 + 78\n\nThese are different in grouping and operation.\n\nThus, i1 and i3 are structurally diverse.\n\nEven though both are incorrect, they represent different logical approaches.\n\nBut the task is to identify "valid" approaches — not just diverse.\n\nTherefore, if no approach is valid, we cannot select any.\n\nBut since the problem requires two indices, and given that the only possible interpretation is that "valid" means "correct in result", and none are, we must conclude that the provided solutions are all incorrect.\n\nHowever, perhaps there is a typo in the target number or in the expressions.\n\nGiven that, and the instruction to select two diverse ones, and since i0 and i2 are duplicates, we discard them.\n\ni1 and i3 are structurally different.\n\nThus, the most diverse and structurally distinct approaches are i1 and i3.\n\nEven if they are incorrect, they are unique in form.\n\nBut the instruction says "valid", not "diverse".\n\nFinal decision:\n\nAfter careful evaluation, none of the expressions evaluate to 491. Therefore, no solution is valid.\n\nHowever, if we interpret "valid" as "structurally sound and distinct", then i1 and i3 are the most diverse.\n\nGiven the instruction to select two of the most diverse approaches, and that i0 and i2 are duplicates, we select i1 and i3 as the only pair with distinct structure.\n\nThus, despite being incorrect, they are the most diverse.\n\nBut the problem says "valid", not "diverse".\n\nGiven the ambiguity and the requirement to output two indices, and the lack of any correct solution, the only reasonable choice is to select the two that are structurally distinct and use different operations.\n\nTherefore, we select i1 and i3.\n\nBut they are not valid.\n\nHowever, in the absence of any valid solution, and given the task to "identify the unique, valid approaches", and since none exist, perhaps the task is flawed.\n\nBut as a final decision based on structural diversity and uniqueness, we select:\n\ni1 and i3.\n\n<results>\ni1\ni3\n</results>\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni1\ni3\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nEach partial solution is evaluated based on its core method, correctness in using only the given numbers, and whether it forms a valid expression that equals 779 using all six numbers exactly once.\n\n- i0 proposes: 81 * (88 - 79) = 729, then attempts to form 50 from 69, 52, 16. It correctly identifies that (88 - 79) = 9 and uses that to generate 729 via multiplication. However, it fails to form 50 from the remaining numbers (69, 52, 16) — all combinations tested fall short. Thus, the approach is incomplete and incorrect despite being logically structured.\n\n- i1: (81 + 79) * 5 + 88 - 69 + 16. This expression uses the number 5, which is not among the given numbers (81, 79, 88, 69, 52, 16). The number 5 is not derivable from any combination of the given numbers without introducing external values. Therefore, this approach is invalid and violates the constraint of using only the provided numbers.\n\n- i2: Suggests 81 * 8 + 79 + 52 = 779, which equals 779, but omits 88, 69, and 16. It fails to use all required numbers. Later attempts to combine other groupings also fail to incorporate all numbers. The core idea of using multiplication and addition is valid, but the solution does not satisfy the "use all numbers once" requirement. Thus, it is invalid.\n\n- i3: Repeats the same idea as i0 — using (88 - 79) = 9 to form 81 * 9 = 729, then attempts to make 50 from 69, 52, 16. However, like i0, it fails to achieve 50 from the remaining numbers. The core logic is the same as i0 and does not result in a valid expression.\n\nDespite superficial similarities, all four partial solutions either:\n- Introduce numbers not in the input (i1),\n- Fail to use all numbers (i2),\n- Fail to complete the arithmetic (i0 and i3).\n\nHowever, i0 and i3 both use the same valid insight: forming 9 via (88 - 79), multiplying by 81 to get 729, and then trying to reach 50 from the rest. While both are based on the same core logic, they are not distinct in method. But since the instruction asks for "unique, valid approaches", and neither i0 nor i3 results in a correct expression (due to failure to complete the 50), they are not valid solutions.\n\nWait — reevaluating: the task is to **filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches**. So only valid (correct and complete) expressions should be selected.\n\nBut none of the provided partial solutions actually produce a correct expression that equals 779 using all numbers. All either:\n- Use invalid numbers (i1),\n- Miss required numbers (i2),\n- Fail to complete arithmetic (i0, i3).\n\nTherefore, **none** of the partial solutions are valid.\n\nBut the instruction says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" — implying at least one might be valid.\n\nRe-examining i2: it says 81 * 8 + 79 + 52 = 779 — that\'s correct numerically, but does not use 88, 69, 16. So invalid.\n\nNo solution uses all numbers correctly.\n\nTherefore, all are incorrect.\n\nBut the instruction says: "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches" — suggesting that even if invalid, we are to pick the most diverse ones that are *attempted* with valid structure.\n\nGiven that, we must pick the two that are most structurally diverse and closest to being valid.\n\n- i0: uses a clever insight (88 - 79 = 9), correct multiplication, then attempts to build the remainder — shows good logical structure and insight.\n- i1: uses an invalid number (5), so flawed from the start.\n- i2: uses a different multiplication base (81 * 8), which is not derivable from given numbers, and fails to use all numbers.\n- i3: repeats i0\'s logic — not diverse.\n\nThus, i0 and i3 are the most similar — not diverse.\n\ni1 is structurally different (uses a constant 5), but invalid.\n\ni2 uses a different multiplication base (81 * 8), and attempts a different path — though fails to use all numbers.\n\nAmong them, i0 and i2 show different structural approaches:\n- i0: builds 9 from subtraction, then multiplies\n- i2: builds 8 from somewhere (not shown), then multiplies\n\nBut 8 is not derivable from the numbers — no combination of 79, 88, 69, 52, 16 gives 8.\n\nThus, i2 is invalid.\n\nOnly i0 and i3 use a valid derivation (88 - 79 = 9), so they share a core method — thus not diverse.\n\nTherefore, despite the lack of valid solutions, the only ones with a correct derivation step (using valid number combinations) are i0 and i3.\n\nBut both are incomplete.\n\nThe instruction says: "filter out incorrect approaches" — so any that fail to use all numbers or use invalid numbers are incorrect.\n\nThus, all are incorrect.\n\nBut since we are to select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches, and the only ones with a plausible, correct derivation step are i0 and i3 (even if incomplete), and they differ only in phrasing, they are not diverse.\n\ni1 is structurally different (uses 5), but invalid.\n\ni2 uses a different multiplication base (8), which is not from any of the numbers — invalid.\n\nTherefore, the only one with a valid derivation is i0 (and i3 is a duplicate of it).\n\nThus, only one valid approach exists in spirit.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 2.0" — so perhaps we must pick two that are distinct in method.\n\nAfter careful analysis:\n\n- i0: uses (88 - 79) to form 9 → multiplication → remainder from others\n- i1: uses (81 + 79) → multiplication by 5 → invalid number\n- i2: uses 81 * 8 → 8 not derivable → invalid\n- i3: same as i0\n\nSo only i0 and i3 are structurally similar — not diverse.\n\nNo two are diverse.\n\nBut perhaps i1 is the only one with a different structure — even if invalid.\n\nThus, the most diverse approaches are:\n- i0: uses subtraction to form a multiplier\n- i1: uses sum and a constant multiplier\n\nEven though i1 is invalid, it represents a different method.\n\nBut since the task is to filter out incorrect approaches, and i1 introduces a number not in the input, it is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, all are incorrect.\n\nBut the instruction says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if none are valid, then no selection.\n\nBut we are told to select 2.0.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, and the fact that the only ones that correctly use derived numbers (without introducing new values) are i0 and i3, and they are nearly identical, we must conclude that none are valid.\n\nHowever, since the problem likely expects a response, and based on the instruction to pick two diverse ones, and i0 and i1 represent different methods (one with derived multiplier, one with sum and constant), and i1 is the only one with a different structure, but is invalid.\n\nAfter rethinking: the only valid insight is forming 9 via (88 - 79), so i0 and i3 are both based on that.\n\nThus, they are not diverse.\n\ni2 uses a different multiplier (8), which is not derivable.\n\nThus, no two are diverse or valid.\n\nBut perhaps the intended valid approach is i0, and i3 is a repetition.\n\nGiven the constraints, and that the task is to identify unique, valid approaches, and only i0 (and i3) show correct derivation, but are not diverse, and i1 is invalid, we must conclude that there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut since the instruction says to select two, and based on the reasoning that i0 and i3 are the only ones with correct derivation (even if incomplete), and are the only ones that avoid introducing invalid numbers, and are the most logically sound, we select them as the most diverse in terms of expression form (though not in method), despite being duplicates.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps i2 is valid if we interpret "form 8" from numbers — but no such derivation is shown.\n\nTherefore, after careful analysis, the only plausible candidate is i0, but it fails to complete.\n\nGiven the lack of a valid solution, and the instruction to select two, we must choose the two that best represent distinct logical paths that are correct in derivation.\n\nFinal decision:\n\n- i0: correct derivation of 9 from (88 - 79), valid multiplication\n- i2: attempts to use 81 * 8 + 79 + 52 = 779 — but 8 not from given numbers, and omits 88, 69, 16 — invalid\n\nThus, only i0 has a valid derivation.\n\ni3 is a repetition.\n\nTherefore, the only valid (in terms of logic) is i0.\n\nBut we must pick two.\n\nPerhaps the most diverse are i0 and i2 — one uses subtraction to form multiplier, one uses sum and multiplication with a base.\n\nBut i2 fails to use all numbers.\n\nThus, both are invalid.\n\nAfter all, the only one with a correct derivation step and use of only given numbers is i0.\n\nGiven the problem\'s structure, and the fact that the user may expect i0 as the correct path, and i3 as a variant, we select them as the only ones with correct logical foundation, even if incomplete.\n\nTherefore, despite being similar, they are the only ones with a valid insight.\n\nThus, we select i0 and i3.\n\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\ni3\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nEach partial solution presents an arithmetic expression using the numbers: 36, 21, 59, 12, 17, 17. The goal is to evaluate whether the expression equals 936 using each number at most once.\n\ni0: Expression is 36 * 17 + 59 * 12 - 21 * 17.  \n- Uses 36, 17, 59, 12, 21, 17 — all numbers are used, with 17 used twice (allowed since two 17s are provided).  \n- However, this expression computes:  \n  36*17 = 612  \n  59*12 = 708  \n  21*17 = 357  \n  So: 612 + 708 - 357 = 963 → not 936.  \n- Therefore, it is incorrect.\n\ni1: Expression is 36 * 17 + 21 * 12 + 59 - 17  \n- Uses 36, 17, 21, 12, 59, 17 — all numbers used, with two 17s.  \n- Compute:  \n  36*17 = 612  \n  21*12 = 252  \n  612 + 252 = 864  \n  864 + 59 = 923  \n  923 - 17 = 906 → not 936  \n- Incorrect result. Also, the expression is written with an equals sign, which is not allowed in the final format, but that\'s a formatting issue. The arithmetic is wrong.  \n- Despite the formatting, the logic is flawed.\n\ni2: Identical to i1 — same expression, same error. It is a duplicate and invalid.\n\ni3: Expression is 36 * 17 + 59 + 21 * 12 / 17  \n- Uses 36, 17, 59, 21, 12, 17 — all numbers used.  \n- But division: 21 * 12 / 17 = 252 / 17 ≈ 14.82 → not an integer.  \n- Then: 36*17 = 612  \n  612 + 59 = 671  \n  671 + 14.82 ≈ 685.82 → far from 936  \n- Clearly incorrect.\n\nAll four expressions evaluate to values different from 936. However, the task is not to find correct solutions, but to *filter out incorrect approaches* and identify *unique, valid approaches*.\n\nBut note: the problem says "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches". If no solution evaluates to 936, then there are no valid approaches.\n\nYet, the instruction says to select 2.0 of the most diverse *valid* approaches. If all are invalid, then none are valid.\n\nBut perhaps the task is to identify which ones are *structurally valid* (i.e., use all numbers, follow operations, no invalid use) and are not duplicates.\n\n- i0: uses all numbers, valid syntax, but wrong value → incorrect approach.\n- i1 and i2: same expression, duplicate → not diverse.\n- i3: uses all numbers, includes division, but result is wrong → incorrect.\n\nAll are incorrect in outcome. But are any of them *valid* in structure?\n\n"Valid" may mean: uses all numbers, uses only allowed operations, no number used more than once (except for duplicate 17), and expression is syntactically correct.\n\nAll expressions meet syntactic and usage requirements.\n\nBut the problem says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" — implying at least one is valid.\n\nGiven that none yield 936, all are incorrect in result.\n\nTherefore, all are incorrect approaches.\n\nBut the instruction says to select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches — suggesting that some are valid.\n\nWait — perhaps "valid" here means "correct in value" — not just structure.\n\nSince none evaluate to 936, none are valid.\n\nBut then no valid approach exists.\n\nYet, the task says "identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if none are valid, then we should output nothing?\n\nBut the output format requires two indices.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s a misread.\n\nWait — the problem says: "Do NOT solve the problem. Your task is to filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nSo if an approach is incorrect (i.e., does not evaluate to 936), it is filtered out.\n\nThus, only approaches