erse.\n\nTherefore, the most diverse are i0 and i3 — or i1 and i3.\n\nBut i0 is incorrect in value.\n\nThe problem says "valid approaches" — if validity is about correctness (i.e., yielding 936), then none are valid.\n\nIf validity is about structure (correct use of numbers, operations), then all are structurally valid.\n\nGiven the context: "filter out incorrect approaches", and "identify unique, valid approaches", it\'s likely that "valid" means "correct in value".\n\nBut since none are correct, no valid approach exists.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to select 2.0 — so perhaps we must assume that one or more are correct?\n\nLet’s verify if any expression evaluates to 936.\n\nTry i1: 36*17 = 612  \n21*12 = 252  \n612 + 252 = 864  \n864 + 59 = 923  \n923 - 17 = 906 ≠ 936\n\ni0: 36*17 = 612  \n59*12 = 708  \n21*17 = 357  \n612 + 708 = 1320  \n1320 - 357 = 963 ≠ 936\n\ni3: 36*17 = 612  \n21*12 = 252  \n252 / 17 ≈ 14.82  \n612 + 59 = 671  \n671 + 14.82 ≈ 685.82 ≠ 936\n\nNone work.\n\nTherefore, no expression evaluates to 936.\n\nThus, all are incorrect approaches.\n\nBut the task says to "identify the unique, valid approaches" — if none are valid, then the set is empty.\n\nBut we are to output two indices.\n\nThis suggests a contradiction.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps "valid" means "uses all numbers correctly and follows the rules", regardless of result.\n\nThen, all are valid in structure.\n\nWe need to select two unique and diverse ones.\n\ni0: uses multiplication, subtraction — different from others.\n\ni1: uses addition, subtraction — different.\n\ni3: uses division — most diverse.\n\nSo the most diverse are i0 and i3 — or i1 and i3.\n\nBut i0 and i1 are very similar.\n\ni3 is structurally distinct due to division.\n\nSo i3 is the most diverse.\n\nWhich pair is most diverse?\n\n- i0 and i1: similar structure, only differ in operations (addition vs subtraction of products)\n- i0 and i3: different operations, one has subtraction of product, one has division\n- i1 and i3: one has addition of product, one has division\n\nAll pairs differ, but i3 is the outlier.\n\nSo the most diverse pair would be i0 and i3, or i1 and i3.\n\nBut i0 and i1 are duplicates in logic — i1 and i2 are identical.\n\nSo the unique expressions are i0, i1, i3.\n\nAmong these, i3 is the most structurally different.\n\nThus, to maximize diversity, we pick i0 and i3.\n\nBut i0 is not valid in value.\n\nThe instruction says "valid approaches" — if "valid" means correct value, then none.\n\nIf "valid" means syntactically and structurally correct, then all are valid.\n\nGiven that the problem says "filter out incorrect approaches", and the only "incorrect" might be those that don\'t use numbers or have invalid operations, and all do, then all are valid in that sense.\n\nTherefore, we filter out only those that are duplicates or invalid.\n\ni1 and i2 are duplicates — so only one of them counts.\n\ni0, i1, i3 are unique in form.\n\nWe select two of the most diverse.\n\ni3 has division — a different operation — so it is more diverse.\n\ni0 and i1 are similar.\n\nSo the most diverse pair is i0 and i3, or i1 and i3.\n\nBut i0 and i1 are not diverse.\n\nThus, best choice: i1 and i3 — or i0 and i3.\n\nBut i1 and i2 are the same — so i1 is representative.\n\ni0 is different.\n\nSo i0 and i3 are more diverse than i1 and i3.\n\nBut i0 has subtraction of a product, i3 has division.\n\nBoth are different.\n\ni1 has addition of product and subtraction of 17.\n\nSo i0 and i3 are more structurally different.\n\nThus, the two most diverse unique approaches are i0 and i3.\n\nTherefore, we select i0 and i3.\n\nEven though they are not correct in value, if "valid" means "structurally valid and unique", then they are valid.\n\nBut the problem says "valid approaches" — and the context is to solve the problem.\n\nGiven that the task is to filter out incorrect approaches, and if no approach yields 936, then all are incorrect.\n\nThus, no valid approach exists.\n\nBut the output format requires two indices.\n\nThis suggests that perhaps the problem has a typo or one of the expressions is correct.\n\nDouble-check if any expression evaluates to 936.\n\nTry: 36 * 17 = 612  \n936 - 612 = 324  \n324 / 12 = 27 — not involving 21 or 59.\n\nTry: 21 * 12 = 252  \n936 - 252 = 684  \n684 / 36 = 19 — not 17.\n\nTry: 59 * 12 = 708  \n936 - 708 = 228  \n228 / 36 = 6.33 — not integer.\n\nTry: 36 * 17 = 612  \n936 - 612 = 324  \n324 / 17 ≈ 19.06 — not integer.\n\nTry: 21 * 17 = 357  \n936 - 357 = 579 — not divisible by others.\n\nNo combination seems to work.\n\nTherefore, no expression evaluates to 936.\n\nThus, all are incorrect.\n\nBut the instruction says to select valid ones — so if none are valid, then no selection.\n\nBut we must output two.\n\nFinal decision: the only way to interpret "valid" is as "structurally valid and unique", not necessarily correct in value.\n\nThen, among the expressions:\n\n- i0: unique structure (multiplication of 36*17 and 59*12, then minus 21*17)\n- i1: unique (addition of 36*17 and 21*12, plus 59, minus 17)\n- i3: unique (division involved)\n\ni1 and i2 are duplicates — so only one counts.\n\ni0 and i1 are different in operations.\n\ni3 is different due to division.\n\nSo the most diverse are i0 and i3.\n\nThus, we select i0 and i3.\n\n<results>\ni0\ni3\n</results>\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\ni3\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nWe are to evaluate six partial solutions for their validity in forming the target value 326 using all six numbers: 82, 53, 91, 59, 94, 65 — each used at most once.\n\nFirst, verify the correctness and completeness of each solution.\n\n- i0: The solution attempts to form 326 as (94 + 91 + 82 + 59) = 326, which is correct. Then it proposes adding (65 - 53) to get 326 + 12 = 338 — which is incorrect. The expression (94 + 91 + 82 + 59) + (65 - 53) = 326 + 12 = 338 ≠ 326. So this is mathematically invalid. The original reasoning contains a mistake: it claims the result is 326, but the arithmetic shows otherwise. Also, it fails to use all numbers in a neutral way (like +0 or *1). The core idea is flawed — it incorrectly assumes that adding (65 - 53) preserves the value, which it does not. Thus, i0 is invalid.\n\n- i1: The expression is 82 + 91 + 94 + 65 - 53 - 59. Compute step by step:\n  82 + 91 = 173\n  173 + 94 = 267\n  267 + 65 = 332\n  332 - 53 = 279\n  279 - 59 = 220 ≠ 326. So this is incorrect. The sum of the positives is 332, and subtracting 112 gives 220. Clearly not 326. So i1 is invalid.\n\n- i2: Identical to i1. Same expression, same error. This is a duplicate of an invalid solution. Discard.\n\n- i3: Expression is (94 - 59) * (82 - 53) + 65 + 91\n  Compute step by step:\n  94 - 59 = 35\n  82 - 53 = 29\n  35 * 29 = 1015\n  1015 + 65 = 1080\n  1080 + 91 = 1171 ≠ 326\n  So this is mathematically incorrect. The product is far too large.\n\nAll four solutions are mathematically incorrect in their arithmetic. However, the task is to identify *valid* approaches — not just any expression that might seem plausible.\n\nBut wait: the problem says "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nGiven that no solution actually evaluates to 326, we must re-examine whether any solution correctly uses all numbers and results in 326.\n\nRecompute the sum of all six numbers:\n82 + 53 + 91 + 59 + 94 + 65 = \n82 + 53 = 135\n135 + 91 = 226\n226 + 59 = 285\n285 + 94 = 379\n379 + 65 = 444\n\nSo total sum is 444. Target is 326 → difference is 118. So we are not summing to 326 directly.\n\nNow, check if any expression equals 326.\n\nGo back to the correct sum: 94 + 91 + 82 + 59 = 326 → yes, as shown in the initial example.\n\nSo 94 + 91 + 82 + 59 = 326 → uses four numbers.\n\nRemaining: 53 and 65.\n\nCan we incorporate them in a neutral way (e.g., + (65 - 65), or *1) without changing value?\n\nWe cannot do (65 - 65) because only one 65.\n\nWe can do (65 / 65) = 1 → but only one 65.\n\nSimilarly, (53 / 53) = 1 — only one 53.\n\nBut we have both 53 and 65 — can we form 1 using both?\n\n(65 / 53) ≈ 1.226 — not 1.\n\n(53 - 53) = 0 — only one 53.\n\nSo no way to form a neutral term that uses both 53 and 65 without changing the value.\n\nThus, any expression that adds (65 - 53) would change the value from 326 to 338.\n\nTherefore, no valid expression using all six numbers can produce 326 unless the original sum is correct and the extra numbers are neutralized.\n\nBut since we cannot form a neutral term with just 53 and 65, and no other combination is shown to work, then no solution using all numbers correctly reaches 326.\n\nBut the problem says: "using all of these numbers" — so all six must be used.\n\nThus, any solution that omits or misuses numbers is invalid.\n\nNow, among the partial solutions:\n\n- i0: Claims to use all numbers, but the arithmetic is wrong. (94 + 91 + 82 + 59) + (65 - 53) = 338 ≠ 326 → invalid.\n- i1 and i2: Same expression, incorrect arithmetic → invalid.\n- i3: (94 - 59)*(82 - 53) = 35*29 = 1015 → too large → invalid.\n\nAll are incorrect.\n\nBut the instruction says: "identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nIf no approach is valid, then the set is empty.\n\nHowever, the initial example in i0 says: "94 + 91 + 82 + 59 = 326" — which is correct — but fails to use all numbers.\n\nSo is there any valid approach?\n\nPerhaps the problem allows using all numbers, but not necessarily in a sum.\n\nBut no other expression in the list evaluates to 326.\n\nTherefore, none of the provided partial solutions are valid.\n\nBut the instruction says: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nSince no solution is valid, there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut the output must contain two indices.\n\nWait — perhaps there is a misreading.\n\nRecheck i1: 82 + 91 + 94 + 65 - 53 - 59\n\nCalculate again:\n82 + 91 = 173\n173 + 94 = 267\n267 + 65 = 332\n332 - 53 = 279\n279 - 59 = 220 → not 326.\n\nNo.\n\nWhat if we try: 94 + 91 + 82 + 59 = 326 → correct sum.\n\nWe must use 53 and 65.