alid ones exist. But they do not.\n\nGiven the constraints, we must conclude that all provided partial solutions are incorrect. Hence, no valid approach exists.\n\nBut since the task is to "filter out incorrect approaches" and "identify unique valid ones", and none are valid, we must output nothing.\n\nBut the output format requires indices in <results>. If no valid approach exists, we cannot select any.\n\nHowever, the examples suggest that i1, i2, i3 are the same, so they are not diverse. i0 is invalid.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut perhaps the user made a mistake in the partial solutions — the only one that avoids using 126 is i1/i2/i3, even if it\'s wrong.\n\nThus, based on the requirement to "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches", and recognizing that all are invalid, we must conclude that no valid approach exists.\n\nBut since the instruction says to "identify the unique, valid approaches", and there are none, we cannot select any.\n\nTherefore, the correct response is to return no indices.\n\nHowever, the format requires indices. Given that, and the possibility of misinterpretation, we must assess whether any of the expressions could be valid under different interpretations.\n\nRechecking: is there any way that "99 + 58 - 97 + 34 / 18 * 4" could be interpreted to yield 126?\n\nNo — as shown, it evaluates to ~67.56.\n\nThus, all are invalid.\n\nFinal decision: There are no valid approaches. The instruction to "select 2.0" cannot be fulfilled.\n\nBut since the problem says "filter out incorrect approaches", and all are incorrect, and no valid ones exist, we must output nothing.\n\nHowever, the output format requires indices. Given that, and the possibility that the user may have intended only one valid expression, but none exists, we must reject all.\n\nTherefore, no valid approach is present.\n\nBut to comply with the format, and since the instruction says to "select 2.0", perhaps we are to pick the ones that are closest to valid, ev