lid incorporation of 49 and 14 is proposed. Thus, it is incomplete and invalid.\n\nNow, group by core logic:\n\n- i1, i3, i5, i6: All use the same incorrect expression → (17 - 7) * (49 - 14) + 6 * 26 → evaluates to 506 → invalid. These are redundant duplicates.\n\n- i0: Trial-and-error with partial insights, fails to reach target or use all numbers → invalid.\n\n- i2: Incorrect arithmetic → invalid.\n\n- i4: Correct expression using all numbers → valid and unique.\n\n- i7: Correctly identifies 26 × 18 = 468 and builds 18 from (17 + 7 - 6), but fails to incorporate 49 and 14. Since it does not use all numbers, it is invalid.\n\nThus, only one valid approach exists: i4.\n\nBut the instruction says to select 5.0 of the most diverse approaches — implying five valid and unique ones. However, after full evaluation, only one partial solution is actually correct and complete.\n\nWait — recheck i7: it says "we must use all numbers" and stops. It does not provide a full expression that includes 49 and 14. So it is incomplete.\n\nTherefore, despite the insight, it does not form a valid expression using all numbers.\n\nOnly i4 provides a complete, correct, and valid expression using all numbers.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 5.0 of the most diverse approaches" — this may be a misstatement. Given that only one valid solution exists, and others are either incorrect or duplicates, we must interpret "diverse" as distinct in method, even if only one is valid.\n\nBut the problem says: "identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nSo we must only select valid ones.\n\nOnly i4 is valid.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 5.0" — this is likely a formatting error or misstatement. We are to select valid and unique approaches, not five.\n\nRe-examining: perhaps i4 and another one?\n\nWait — is there another valid expression?\n\ni4: 49*(26-17) + 14 + 6 + 7 = 468 → correct.\n\nIs there another valid expression?\n\ni7: 26 × (17 + 7 - 6) = 468 → correct value, but does not use 49 and 14 →