rect.\n\n- i2: Proposes (74 + 56) * 5 - 9 + 71 - 44 + 59. It introduces a "5" which is not among the given numbers (we have 74, 44, 56, 9, 59, 71). This is invalid because it uses an extra number not in the original set. Also, the expression uses 5, which is not allowed. Thus, it is incorrect and invalid.\n\n- i3, i4, i6: These are variations of the same incorrect expression (the one with (74+56)*9 - (59+71-44)), which we have already verified as incorrect. They do not represent new or valid logic.\n\n- i7: Suggests (74 + 56) * 5 - 9 + 71 - 44 + 59 — again, introduces a "5" not in the original numbers. Invalid.\n\nNow, consider the correct insight from i6:  \nIt correctly identifies that 56 * 9 = 504, and 504 - 12 = 492. Then it finds that 71 - 59 = 12. So:\n56 * 9 - (71 - 59) = 504 - 12 = 492  \nRemaining numbers: 74 and 44. But this expression does not use 74 and 44.  \nSo it fails to use all numbers — it only uses 56, 9, 71, 59. Missing 74 and 44.  \nThus, this is incomplete and invalid.\n\nBut wait — is there a way to use all numbers?\n\nLet’s re-express the correct path from the valid logic:\nWe need to use all six numbers.\n\nFrom i6’s idea: 56 * 9 = 504 → 504 - 12 = 492  \nCan we form 12 from others? Yes: 74 - 71 = 3, 59 - 44 = 15 → 15 - 3 = 12  \nSo: (59 - 44) - (74 - 71) = 15 - 3 = 12  \nThus: 56 * 9 - ((59 - 44) - (74 - 71)) = 504 - 12 = 492\n\nNow check: numbers used — 56, 9, 59, 44, 74, 71 → all six numbers, each used once.\n\nThis is a valid expression.\n\nBut is this explicitly presented in any of the partial solutions?\n\nNo — it is only implied in i6’s reasoning, but not written as a complete expression.\n\nSimilarly, no solution in the list actually presents this correct and complete expression.\n\nAll the given partial solutions either:\n- Repeat the same invalid expression (i0, i1, i5, i3, i4, i6)\n- Introduce invalid numbers (like 5 in i2, i7)\n- Fail to use all numbers\n- Are incomplete or incorrect in evaluation\n\nTherefore, none of the provid