\n\nCan we make 15 from 59, 25, and 2?\n\n- 25 - 10 = 15 → can we make 10? 59 - 49 = 10 → no obvious path  \n- 59 - 25 = 34 → too big  \n- 25 - 2 = 23 → no  \n- (59 + 2) = 61 → no  \n- (25 + 2) = 27 → no\n\nNo valid combination of the remaining numbers makes 15.\n\nThus, no solution in the provided partials is valid.\n\nBut the instruction says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" — implying that at least one may be valid.\n\nHowever, all expressions provided are mathematically incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the set of partial solutions contains no valid expressions.\n\nBut since the instruction asks to select "5.0 of the most diverse approaches" (which implies a selection of valid ones), and none are valid, we must conclude that no valid approach exists in the list.\n\nBut the instruction says: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nGiven that all provided expressions are incorrect, there are zero valid approaches.\n\nHowever, the instruction says "select 5.0", which is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, we must interpret this as: among the provided partial solutions, which ones represent *distinct* and *correct* approaches — even if not fully evaluated.\n\nBut none are correct.\n\nThus, all are incorrect.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut the output format requires 5 indices.\n\nThis implies a possible misinterpretation.\n\nWait — perhaps one of the expressions, when re-evaluated, is correct?\n\nRecheck i2, i3, i5: (59 + 2) * 22 + 84 / 2 + 25\n\n= 61 * 22 = 1342  \n84 / 2 = 42  \n1342 + 42 = 1384  \n1384 + 25 = 1409 → not 939.\n\nNo.\n\nIs there any expression that evaluates to 939?\n\nTry: 84 * (22 / 2) = 924  \n939 - 924 = 15  \nCan we get 15 from 59, 25, and 2?\n\n(59 - 25) = 34 → no  \n(25 - 2) = 23 → no  \n(59 - 2) = 57 → no  \n(25 + 2) = 27 → no\n\nNo.\n\nTry: 25 * 2 = 50  \n59 * 2 = 118  \n84 * 2 = 168 → all 2s used → total = 50 + 118 + 168 = 336 + 22 = 358 → no.\n\nNo combination yields 939.\n\nTherefore