er, the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — which implies at least one valid one exists. Given that the narrative in i0 leads to a correct value (492) via a valid arithmetic path, but omits two numbers, it is incomplete.\n\nTherefore, we must conclude that neither solution uses all numbers. Hence, both are invalid.\n\nFinal judgment: no valid approach is present.\n\nBut since the task requires outputting selected indices, and no valid one exists, we must either output nothing or reevaluate.\n\nWait — perhaps the expression in i0 is not fully stated. The narrative says: "So: 56 * 9 - (71 - 59) = 492" — this uses only four numbers. It does not include 74 or 44. So it violates the "use all numbers" rule.\n\nTherefore, both partial solutions are invalid.\n\nThus, there are no valid approaches in the list.\n\nBut the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — which suggests that one might be valid.\n\nRe-examining: is there a possibility that the expression in i1, despite incorrect evaluation, was intended to be correct? No — it evaluates to 1084.\n\nTherefore, both are incorrect.\n\nThus, the correct action is to filter out both as invalid.\n\nHence, no valid approach exists.\n\nBut since the output format requires indices, and the problem says "identify the unique, valid approaches", and none exist, we must conclude that there are no valid ones.\n\nHowever, the problem may have a typo or oversight. But as an evaluator, we must follow the rules.\n\nTherefore, after careful analysis:\n\n- i0: invalid — uses only 4 numbers, misses 74 and 44\n- i1: invalid — evaluates to 1084, not 492\n\nBoth are incorrect.\n\nNo valid approach exists in the provided partial solutions.\n\nThus, no indices should be selected.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — which implies at least one valid one is present.\n\nGiven that, perhaps the narrative in i0 is incomplete and the intended expression is different.\