 \n(54 - 33) * (23 - 16) + 95 - 53  \nCompute step-by-step:  \n(21) * (7) = 147  \n147 + 95 = 242  \n242 - 53 = 189 → Not 100.\n\nSo i0 evaluates to 189, not 100. It uses subtraction and multiplication in a structured way, grouping differences and combining them linearly.\n\nNow analyze i1:  \n(54 - 33) * (16 + 23) - (95 - 53)  \nCompute:  \n(21) * (39) = 819  \n(95 - 53) = 42  \n819 - 42 = 777 → Not 100.\n\ni1 evaluates to 777, clearly not 100. It uses a similar structure: a product of two differences, but one is a sum (16+23), and the other is a subtraction (95-53). The core method is structurally similar to i0 — both use (54 - 33) as a common factor, and then combine the remaining numbers via basic arithmetic operations.\n\nDespite superficial differences (one uses sum, the other difference), both rely on the same core pattern:  \n- Extract (54 - 33) = 21  \n- Multiply it by a combination of the remaining numbers (either (23 - 16) or (16 + 23))  \n- Then adjust with (95 - 53)\n\nThe operations are algebraically similar and use the same foundational logic. Thus, they are not unique in approach — they are variations of the same structural method, differing only in the grouping of the second operand.\n\nSince the problem asks to identify a unique, valid approach (and both are invalid in outcome), and they share the same underlying method, one is redundant.\n\nTherefore, neither solution produces 100. However, the instruction is to identify a unique, valid approach — meaning one that is structurally distinct and potentially correct (even if not verified numerically). But in this case, both are invalid numerically and share the same logic.\n\nGiven that, and the requirement to select one of the most diverse approaches, we must conclude that both are structurally identical in method, differing only in the arithmetic combination of the second group. Hence, they are not diverse.\n\nSince no valid solution (that evaluates to 100) is present, and both are incorrect, the on