cannot select any. But since the format requires outputting indices, and only incorrect approaches are present, we must infer that the task may have been misaligned.\n\nHowever, re-evaluating: the only logical path toward 644 is 28 × 23 = 644. Can 23 be formed from the remaining numbers: 19, 19, 37, 26, 40?\n\nTry: (37 + 26) = 63; (40 - 19) = 21; (19 + 19) = 38 → 38 - 15 = 23? Can we make 15?\n\n(40 - 37) = 3; (26 - 19) = 7; 3 + 7 = 10 → not helpful.\n\n(37 - 26) = 11; (19 - 11) = 8 → not 23.\n\n(40 - 19) = 21; 21 + 19 = 40 → no.\n\n(19 + 19) = 38; 38 - 15 = 23 → can we make 15? (26 - 11) → no.\n\nNo valid path to 23.\n\nAlternative: try 40 × 16 = 640 → 644 - 640 = 4 → can we make 16 and 4?\n\n(37 - 26) = 11 → not 16.\n\n(19 + 19) = 38 → too big.\n\n(40 + 28) = 68 → 68 × 9 = 612 → 644 - 612 = 32 → not helpful.\n\n(37 × 19) = 703 → too big.\n\n(40 × 19) = 760 → 760 - 37 = 723 → 723 - 28 = 695 → too big.\n\n(37 × 19) = 703 → 703 - 40 = 663 → 663 - 28 = 635 → 635 + 19 = 654 → no.\n\nNo valid combination found.\n\nTherefore, no valid expression exists using only the given numbers to reach 644. However, the problem states "create an expression", implying one exists.\n\nBut since the partial solutions both contain the same invalid step (introducing 10), they are incorrect and non-diverse.\n\nThus, after careful comparison: both i0 and i1 are incorrect, use the same flawed method, and introduce an invalid number (10). They are duplicates in logic and invalid.\n\nThere is no valid, unique, correct approach among the given partial solutions.\n\nTherefore, no valid approach can be selected.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — but since all are invalid, and no valid one exists, we must conclude that the provided partial solutions do not contain any correct or valid approach.\n\nThus, the output should reflect that no valid approach is present — but since the format requires indices, and only incorrect ones are given, we must reject a