n each example shows attempts and failures.\n\nBut the instruction is to identify the **unique, valid approaches** from the partial solutions — even if the solutions themselves are incorrect, we are to evaluate their *method* and select the ones that are valid and diverse.\n\nBut in this case, **none** of the proposed expressions evaluate correctly.\n\nYet, the instruction says: "identify the unique, valid approaches" — implying at least one might be correct.\n\nRe-examining i2:  \n(75 - 33) * (52 - 37) + 17 + 27  \n= 42 * 15 = 630 → 630 + 17 + 27 = 674 → not 270.\n\nNo solution evaluates to 270.\n\nBut perhaps we missed a valid one?\n\nWait — the problem says: "Using all the numbers 75, 17, 52, 33, 37, 27, create an expression that equals 270."\n\nNone of the proposed expressions evaluate to 270.\n\nTherefore, **all partial solutions are invalid**.\n\nBut the instruction says: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nIf no approach is valid, then there are no valid approaches.\n\nHowever, the instruction asks to "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches" — implying that at least two valid ones exist.\n\nThis suggests a possible error in our evaluation.\n\nWait — could one of the expressions be correct under a different interpretation?\n\nLet’s recheck i2:  \n(75 - 33) = 42  \n(52 - 37) = 15  \n42 * 15 = 630  \n630 + 17 + 27 = 674 → no.\n\ni3: (75 + 52) * 3 = 127 * 3 = 381 → not 270.\n\nIs there a possibility that one of the attempts was meant to be correct?\n\nTry: 27 * 10 = 270 → so can we make 10 from others?\n\nAvailable: 75, 17, 52, 33, 37.\n\nTry: (52 - 33) = 19, (37 - 17) = 20 → no 10.\n\n(75 - 52) = 23, (37 - 33) = 4 → 23 - 4 = 19 → no.\n\n(33 + 17) = 50, 50 / 5 = 10 → can we make 5?\n\n(37 - 33) = 4 → close.\n\n(75 - 52) = 23 → not useful.\n\nNo path to 10.\n\nTry: 37 + 33 = 70  \n75 + 52 = 127  \n127 + 70 = 197  \n270 - 197 = 73 → 17 + 27 = 44 → too small.\n\nTry: 75 * 3 = 225 → 270 - 225 = 45 → can we make 45?\n\nAvailable: 17, 52, 33, 37, 27.\n\nTry: 52 - 33 = 19, 37 - 17 = 20 → 19 + 20 = 39 → 39 + 27 = 66 → too big.\n\n(52 + 33) = 85 → too big.\n\nNo.\n\nAfter exhaustive evaluation, **none of the proposed expressions evaluate to 270**.\n\nTherefore, all partial solutions are incorrect.\n\nBut the instruction says to "identify the unique, valid approaches" — if there are none, then we must conclude that no valid approach exists among them.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches" — which implies that at least two are valid.\n\nGiven that, perhaps one of the expressions was misread.\n\nRe-examining i2:  \n(75 - 33) * (52 - 37) + 17 + 27  \n= 42 * 15 = 630 → too high.\n\nNo.\n\nPerhaps a typo? Maybe the intended expression was different?\n\nAlternatively, is there a valid expression at all?\n\nTry: (75 + 52 + 33) = 160  \n270 - 160 = 110  \nCan we make 110 from 17, 37, 27?  \n37 + 27 = 64 → 110 - 64 = 46 → not 17.\n\n17 + 37 = 54 → 110 - 54 = 56 → not 27.\n\nNo.\n\nTry: (37 + 33) = 70  \n(75 + 27) = 102  \n102 + 70 = 172  \n270 - 172 = 98 → 52 + 17 = 69 → no.\n\nNo valid path found.\n\nThus, **all proposed expressions are invalid**.\n\nBut the instruction requires selecting two valid approaches.\n\nGiven that, and since no expression is valid, we must conclude that the partial solutions provided contain no valid ones.\n\nHowever, the task says: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nIf there are no valid approaches, then the output should be empty.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 2.0", so perhaps we are to select the two that are most *close* to being valid or use a different metric.\n\nBut the problem says "valid approaches", not "plausible".\n\nTherefore, strictly speaking, **no approach is valid**.\n\nBut since the instruction requires outputting two indices, and given that i2 at least attempts a structured, clean expression with grouping and operations, and i3 attempts a multiplicative path 