efore, after deep analysis, we conclude that there are no valid approaches.\n\nHowever, to fulfill the output format, and if we interpret "valid" as "correct in structure and arithmetic", then none are valid.\n\nBut perhaps the problem is to use all numbers, and the only possible way is to have a neutral term.\n\nSince (65 - 53) = 12, and 326 + 12 = 338, not 326.\n\nNo.\n\nTherefore, no valid solution.\n\nFinal output: no valid approaches.\n\nBut since the instruction says to select two, and we must, we select the two that are most structurally different and attempt to use all numbers — i0 and i3 — even though both are incorrect.\n\nBut they are not valid.\n\nGiven that the task is to identify valid approaches, and there are none, we must reject all.\n\nBut the only possible way to proceed is to output two indices that are unique in approach.\n\ni0: sum of four numbers + (65-53) — incorrect\ni1: sum of four + subtraction of two — incorrect\ni3: multiplication and addition — incorrect\n\ni0 and i3 are structurally different.\n\ni1 and i2 are duplicates.\n\nSo the most diverse are i0 and i3.\n\nEven though both are invalid, i0 has the correct base sum, and i3 has a different operation.\n\nTherefore, despite being incorrect, they are the most diverse in approach.\n\nBut the instruction says "valid".\n\nAfter re-reading: "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches"\n\nSo only valid ones should be selected.\n\nSince there are none, the set is empty.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 2.0", which implies there are two.\n\nThis suggests that one of the solutions might be correct.\n\nLet me recompute i1: 82 + 91 + 94 + 65 - 53 - 59\n\n82+91=173\n173+94=267\n267+65=332\n332-53=279\n279-59=220 → not 326.\n\nNo.\n\nWhat if it\'s 82 + 91 + 94 + 65 - 53 - 59 = 326? No.\n\nPerhaps the numbers are different.\n\nPerhaps the target is 220, not 326.\n\nBut the problem says 326.\n\nAnother possibility: (82 + 91) = 173; (94 + 59) = 153; 173+153=326 — yes