80 - 73) = 7 → 7 - 5 = 2 → not 3.\n\n(68 - 44) = 24; 24 / 8 = 3 → no 8.\n\nTry: 80 + 44 = 124; 124 - 100 = 24; 24 - 17 = 7 → not helpful.\n\nTry: 73 + 44 = 117; 117 - 17 = 100 → already have 100.\n\nTry: 68 + 44 = 112; 114 - 112 = 2 → need to generate 2.\n\n(80 - 73) = 7; (100 - 80) = 20; (73 - 68) = 5 → 20 / 5 = 4 → not 2.\n\n(80 - 73) = 7; (68 - 44) = 24 → 24 / 12 = 2 → no 12.\n\nNo obvious path.\n\nThus, no valid expression is known.\n\nTherefore, all partial solutions are invalid or incorrect.\n\nBut the only one that uses only the given numbers is i1.\n\ni0, i2, i3 all introduce "1" → invalid.\n\nTherefore, only i1 is structurally valid.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 2.0" — so perhaps we are to pick two that are unique in method.\n\nBut there is only one valid method.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps i1 is the only one that is valid, and the others are invalid, so we cannot select two.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches".\n\nThis implies that there are at least two valid ones.\n\nBut there are not.\n\nTherefore, we must conclude that the only valid approach (in terms of structure) is i1.\n\nBut since the instruction says "2.0", and we must output two, and only one exists, perhaps we made a mistake.\n\nWait — in i0, it says: "Wait — we don\'t have 10. Let\'s reevaluate." — this shows it is aware of the invalid number.\n\nIn i2 and i3, they explicitly write "(17 + 1)" — clearly invalid.\n\ni1 has no such invalid number.\n\nTherefore, i1 is the only valid expression in terms of number usage.\n\nThus, only one valid approach.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 2.0", so perhaps it\'s a typo.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps "valid" means "correct in value" — but none are.\n\nTherefore, no valid approach.\n\nBut that contradicts the instruction.\n\nGiven the constraints, and that only i1 avoids introducing invalid numbers, and all others do, we conclude that i1 is the only valid approach.\n\nHowever, since the instruction says "select 2.0