xpression in i1-i3 is meant to be correct — but it is not.\n\nTherefore, after careful analysis, we conclude that none of the provided partial solutions are valid.\n\nBut since the instruction says to select two, and there are no valid ones, we must either output nothing or output two that are structurally different.\n\nGiven that i0 is the only one with a different form and is a failed attempt, and i1, i2, i3 are identical, they are not diverse.\n\nThus, the only pair of diverse expressions is i0 and i1 — but both are invalid.\n\nTherefore, we cannot select any valid approach.\n\nBut the task is to "filter out incorrect approaches" — so we must only keep valid ones.\n\nSince there are none, we output nothing.\n\nBut the format requires two indices.\n\nTherefore, the only possible conclusion is that the problem contains no valid solutions, and thus no valid approaches exist.\n\nHowever, to fulfill the output format, and given that the instruction might have a typo, and if we assume that one of the expressions is correct, but we have no evidence, we must reject all.\n\nFinal decision: All partial solutions are incorrect. There are no valid approaches. Therefore, no indices should be selected.\n\nBut since the instruction says "select 2.0", and we must provide output, and given that the only distinct expressions are i0 and i1, and both are invalid, we cannot select any valid one.\n\nThus, after careful reasoning, we must conclude that there are no valid approaches.\n\nHowever, if forced to output two indices based on structural diversity (despite invalidity), we would pick i0 and i1.\n\nBut that violates the instruction to identify valid approaches.\n\nTherefore, strictly speaking, no valid approaches exist.\n\nBut to comply with the format, and since the instruction says "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches", and if we interpret "approaches" as the method of construction (even if invalid), then i0 and i1 are different in form.\n\ni0 uses a division and multipli