ce no solution is valid, and all are flawed, the only possibility is that the problem has no solution under the constraints — but that contradicts the premise.\n\nBut note: i6 is the only one that attempts a full expression with all numbers. It uses all six numbers exactly once. However, it evaluates to 1392, not 826.\n\nThus, no solution is valid.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches among the partial solutions.\n\nBut the instruction says to "select 4.0 of the most diverse approaches" — implying at least four valid ones.\n\nThis suggests a misinterpretation.\n\nRe-examining: perhaps one of the solutions is correct.\n\nTry: 92 * 9 = 828 → 828 - 2 = 826.\n\nCan we form 9 and 2 from the others?\n\nWe have: 49, 17, 41, 15, 37.\n\n(17 - 15) = 2 → good.\n\nCan we form 9?\n\nTry: (49 - 41) = 8 → 8 + (1) = 9 → need 1.\n\nCan we make 1? (37 - 36)? (49 - 48)? No.\n\n(37 - 36) — no 36.\n\n(41 - 40)? No.\n\n(49 + 37) = 86 → 86 / 17 ≈ 5.06 → no.\n\n(49 + 15) = 64 → 64 / 37 ≈ 1.73 → no.\n\nNo.\n\nTry: 15 * 41 = 615; 826 - 615 = 211.\n\nCan we make 211 from 92, 49, 17, 37?\n\n92 + 49 = 141; 141 + 37 = 178; 178 + 17 = 195 → too small.\n\n92 + 17 = 109; 109 + 37 = 146; 146 + 49 = 195 → same.\n\nNo.\n\nTry: 49 * 15 = 735; 826 - 735 = 91 → 92 - 1 = 91 → can we make 1?\n\n92 - 91 = 1 → so 49 * 15 + (92 - 91) = 826 → need to make 91 from others.\n\nOthers: 17, 41, 37.\n\nCan we make 91?\n\n41 + 37 = 78; 78 + 17 = 95 → too big.\n\n41 + 17 = 58; 58 + 37 = 95 → same.\n\n37 + 17 = 54 → no.\n\nNo.\n\nAfter exhaustive checking, no valid expression using all numbers exactly once evaluates to 826.\n\nTherefore, all partial solutions are incorrect.\n\nBut the instruction says to "identify the unique, valid approaches" — if none exist, then the output should be empty.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to "select 4.0 of the most diverse approaches", implying four.\n\nThis suggests that perhaps one of the solutions is actually correct and the others are flawed.\n\nRecheck i6: 92 * 15 - 49 +