que approaches. It is likely a typo or misstatement.\n\nGiven the requirement to output four indices, and the absence of a fourth valid and diverse approach, the most reasonable selection is to include one from each of the three distinct groups, with one duplicate to meet the count, though this reduces diversity.\n\nThus, the selected indices are i0, i2, i7, and i6 (a duplicate of i2, but different index).\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\ni2\ni7\ni6\n</results>\n', '<reasoning>\nAll the provided partial solutions attempt to construct an expression equal to 644 using the numbers 19, 19, 37, 26, 40, and 28, each used at most once. However, each solution contains a critical flaw: they introduce numbers not in the original set (e.g., 7, 10), which violates the constraint of using only the given numbers.\n\nFor instance, solutions i0, i1, i2, i3, i4, i5, i6, and i7 all contain expressions like "+ 40 * 28 / 10" or "+ 40 * 28 / 7", which introduce the numbers 10 or 7. These are not among the original set of numbers (19, 19, 37, 26, 40, 28), and thus such expressions are invalid under the rules.\n\nThe core idea in several of these solutions — particularly that 28 × 23 = 644 — is valid and mathematically sound. However, the attempt to generate 23 from the remaining numbers (19, 19, 37, 26, 40) using only arithmetic operations is not successful in any of the given attempts. None of the solutions provide a valid expression that uses only the given numbers without introducing extraneous constants.\n\nTherefore, every partial solution is invalid due to the introduction of numbers not in the original set. There are no correct or valid approaches among the given partial solutions that strictly adhere to the constraints.\n\nSince all solutions are incorrect (due to introducing numbers not in the input), there are no valid or unique approaches to select.\n\nHowever, the instruction requires selecting 4.0 of the most diverse approaches — even if all are invalid. Given that, we must identify which ones show the most distinct flawed attempts (i.e., different invalid strategies), to fulfill the diversity requirement.\n\n- i0, i1, i2, i3, i4, i5, i6, i7 all share the same pattern: they attempt to build 644 via multiplication (e.g., 40×28 or 28×23) and then introduce a divisor like 10 or 7 to adjust the result.\n- The only variation is in the introduced constant (10 or 7) and the phrasing, but the underlying method is identical: introducing a new number not in the list.\n- All are fundamentally flawed and use the same invalid logic.\n\nThus, despite superficial differences in wording or slight rephrasing, all solutions are duplicates in approach — they all introduce extraneous numbers (7 or 10) not in the original set.\n\nTherefore, there are no unique or valid approaches. All are incorrect.\n\nHowever, since the task requires selecting 4.0 of the most diverse approaches, and no valid ones exist, we must choose four that represent different expressions of the same flawed method, even if they are not valid.\n\nThe most diverse ones are:\n- i0: first to introduce /7 (though not present)\n- i1: introduces /10\n- i2: introduces /10\n- i3: introduces /10\n- i4: tries a different path (e.g., 644 ÷ 19, etc.)\n- i5: repeats 28×23 idea\n- i6: same as i5\n- i7: tries 40×16\n\nAmong these, i4 and i7 show slightly different reasoning paths:\n- i4 explores division of 644 by 19, 28, 40\n- i7 tries 40×16 = 640 and looks for +4\n\nThese are the only ones that deviate slightly from the "28×23" or "divisor trick" path.\n\nThus, to satisfy the requirement of selecting 4 diverse (but still incorrect) approaches, we pick:\n- i0: introduces /7\n- i1: introduces /10\n- i4: explores division and alternative paths\n- i7: explores 40×16 + 4\n\nThese represent four distinct flawed strategies, even if all are invalid.\n\nFinal selection: i0, i1, i4, i7.\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\ni1\ni4\ni7\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nWe are given six partial solutions, and our task is to ev