t requires selecting 4.0 indices — which suggests the evaluator must pick four.\n\nGiven that, and since the instruction says "prune solutions that might lead to the same outcome", we must group by core logic.\n\n- i0, i1, i6: All use simple multiplication and addition with all numbers, but result in low values → incorrect, but same flawed logic (additive combinations).\n- i2, i3, i4, i5: All use (59+2)*22 + 84/2 + 25 → same expression, same error → redundant, invalid.\n- i7: Uses (84-22)*25 + 59*2 → different structure, but still incorrect.\n\nThus, the only distinct *types* of approaches are:\n1. Additive combinations with multiplication (i0, i1, i6)\n2. Multiplicative group with (59+2)*22 and division (i2, i3, i4, i5)\n3. Subtractive multiplication (i7)\n\nThese are three distinct structural approaches, all of which are invalid.\n\nBut the instruction says to select 4.0 of the most diverse approaches — implying there are at least four.\n\nSince all are invalid, but we must provide four indices, and the only way to satisfy the output format is to pick four distinct ones, we pick the four that represent the most diverse *structural* approaches, even if mathematically incorrect.\n\nThus, we select:\n- i0 (basic additive multiplication)\n- i1 (same as i0, but different phrasing — redundant)\n- i2 (the (59+2)*22 + ...) approach\n- i7 (subtractive multiplication)\n\nBut i0 and i1 are duplicates. So diversity is low.\n\nTo maximize diversity in structure:\n- i0: simple multiplication and addition\n- i6: same pattern, different grouping\n- i2: uses addition inside parentheses and division\n- i7: uses subtraction and multiplication\n\nThus, four distinct structural forms.\n\nEven though all are invalid, they represent different approaches.\n\nTherefore, we select the four indices that represent the most structurally diverse incorrect approaches.\n\nFinal selection: i0, i6, i2, i7.\n\nNote: All are incorrect, but they are structurally distinct.\n\n</reasoning>\n<results>\n