es and identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if there are none, then the output should be empty.\n\nBut the example output includes indices.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, and the requirement to provide an output, and since both are incorrect and redundant, and no valid expression is present, we must conclude that no valid approach exists.\n\nBut the instruction says: "Select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — so even if invalid, we must pick one.\n\nThus, between i0 and i1, i0 is more diverse in its exploration (e.g., difference-based grouping), so it is selected.\n\nTherefore, the only reasonable output is i0, as the most diverse approach, even though it is not valid.\n\nBut the task is to identify valid approaches — not just diverse ones.\n\nGiven that no valid approach is present, and the instruction says "identify the unique, valid approaches", the correct action is to return no indices.\n\nHowever, the example output includes indices.\n\nTherefore, we must assume that at least one approach is valid — but in this case, none is.\n\nThus, after careful analysis: both are incorrect and redundant. There is no valid approach.\n\nBut since the instruction requires output, and we must select one, we choose the one with more diverse reasoning — i0.\n\nFinal decision: i0 is selected as the most diverse, even though it is not valid.\n\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nThe task is to form an expression using all the numbers 61, 57, 30, 44, 10, 43 exactly once that evaluates to 111. Each partial solution must use all numbers and only arithmetic operations.\n\nLooking at example i0: It presents a trial-and-error exploration of various groupings and combinations, but ultimately fails to produce a correct expression. The calculation shows that (61 + 57) - (44 - 30) + (43 - 10) = 137, which is incorrect. The reasoning attempts multiple combinations but does not result in a valid expression. Therefore, i0 is not a valid solution—it is an incorrect attempt