e wrong, we must conclude that no valid approach exists.\n\nTherefore, no indices should be selected.\n\nHowever, the problem says "from a set of partial solutions", and if all are incorrect, then none are valid.\n\nThus, the correct action is to reject all.\n\nBut the output format requires indices.\n\nGiven that, and since the task is to identify unique, valid approaches, and there are none, we must return no indices.\n\nBut the example output includes indices.\n\nTherefore, re-evaluating: perhaps the expression is actually valid?\n\nRecompute:\n(99 - 94) = 5  \n(82 - 75) = 7  \n5 * 7 = 35  \n14 * 5 = 70  \n35 + 70 = 105 → not 104.\n\nSo it\'s off by 1.\n\nNo valid solution is present.\n\nThus, all are incorrect.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches.\n\nFinal decision: since all solutions are incorrect and identical, they are not valid. There are no valid approaches to select.\n\nThus, no indices should be output.\n\nBut the instruction says "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — implying at least one is valid.\n\nGiven that, and that no valid one exists, we must conclude that the provided partial solutions are all incorrect.\n\nTherefore, no valid approach is present.\n\nHence, no indices.\n\nBut if we must output something, and only one is given, and it\'s the only one, then perhaps we output it — but it\'s wrong.\n\nThe task is to filter out incorrect approaches — so we must exclude them.\n\nThus, the set is empty.\n\nTherefore, the output should be no indices.\n\nBut the example output has indices.\n\nRe-examining the problem: it says "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches".\n\nIf there are no valid approaches, then output nothing.\n\nThus:\n\n<reasoning>\nAll provided partial solutions are identical and evaluate to 105, not 104. Therefore, they are all incorrect. There is no diversity in approach since both solutions use the exact same expression. No valid approach exists in the set. The expression (99 - 94) * (