here are no valid approaches.\n\nBut the problem may have a typo or missing solution.\n\nGiven that, and since the instruction is to identify valid approaches, and none are valid, we must return no indices.\n\nBut the format requires output.\n\nRe-examining: the only expression that comes close is i2 and i3, which are identical and clearly invalid.\n\ni0 and i1 are also invalid.\n\nThus, no valid approach exists.\n\nTherefore, the correct action is to return no indices.\n\nBut the output format says "each index on a new line".\n\nSo if no valid approach, output nothing?\n\nBut the instruction says "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — implying at least one is valid.\n\nWait — perhaps we misread i2 and i3.\n\ni2: (95 - 36) * 4 + 32 + 18 - 29  \n= (59) * 4 = 236  \n236 + 32 = 268  \n268 + 18 = 286  \n286 - 29 = 257 → not 114.\n\nNo.\n\nIs there any expression that evaluates to 114?\n\nTry: 36 * 4 = 144  \n144 - 32 = 112  \n112 + 18 = 130 → too high  \n130 - 29 = 101 → 101 + 95 = 196 → no\n\nTry: 95 + 32 = 127  \n127 - 18 = 109  \n109 + 4 = 113  \n113 + 1 = 114 → need 1 from 36 and 29 → 36/36=1 → but then 36 used twice? No.\n\n95 + 32 = 127  \n127 - 18 = 109  \n109 + (36 / 36) = 110 → not 114\n\nNo.\n\nAnother: 29 + 36 = 65  \n95 - 65 = 30  \n30 + 32 = 62  \n62 + 18 = 80 → +4 = 84 → no\n\nNo combination seems to yield 114.\n\nThus, no valid expression exists.\n\nTherefore, all partial solutions are incorrect.\n\nFinal decision: no valid approach exists among the given ones.\n\nBut since the instruction says to "identify the unique, valid approaches", and there are none, we must return no indices.\n\nHowever, the output format requires indices.\n\nGiven that, and since the task is to filter out incorrect ones, and all are incorrect, there are no valid approaches.\n\nThus, the selected indices are none.\n\nBut the format requires one or more indices.\n\nPerhaps the problem allows for a valid expression that we missed.\n\nWait — is it possible that the expression in