e, but it uses only two numbers and introduces a 3 not in the list. It also omits 17, 33, 37, 27 — violating the "use all numbers" rule.\n\nThus, invalid.\n\nConclusion: All partial solutions are invalid.\n\nTherefore, no valid approach exists in the list.\n\nBut since the task requires selecting one, and assuming that the problem might have a typo or one of the expressions is correct, we must check if any expression, despite being written incorrectly, could be interpreted as valid.\n\nNo.\n\nFinal decision: All are incorrect. But if we must pick one that is structurally most plausible (even if flawed), i2 uses a structured algebraic form with differences and additions — it is the most mathematically structured and uses only the given numbers without introducing external constants.\n\nHowever, it evaluates incorrectly.\n\nThus, it is not valid.\n\nTherefore, after thorough analysis, **no valid approach exists** in the provided partial solutions.\n\nBut since the instruction requires outputting indices, and given that the problem may have a typo or missing solution, and based on the requirement to "identify the unique, valid approaches", and since **none are valid**, we must conclude that there are no valid ones.\n\nHowever, if forced to pick one that is *closest* in structure and uses only the given numbers without introducing external values, i2 is the only one that does so — even though it is wrong.\n\nBut the instruction says "valid", not "plausible".\n\nTherefore, strictly speaking, **no valid approach exists**.\n\nBut since the output format requires indices, and the instruction says "select 1 of the most diverse approaches", perhaps the intended correct one is missing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps i2 was meant to be (75 + 33) * 3 + 17 + 27 — but that’s not what is written.\n\nGiven that all are invalid, and no one is valid, we must conclude that the task has no solution in the provided partials.\n\nBut to comply with the format, and if we interpret "valid" as "us