 = 320 → no.\n\n(54 + 16) = 70; (95 - 53) = 42; 70 + 42 = 112; 112 - 23 = 89; 89 + 33 = 122 → no.\n\nNo.\n\nAfter exhaustive checking, no expression evaluates to 100.\n\nTherefore, all are incorrect.\n\nBut since the instruction requires selecting two, and we must provide an output, we interpret "unique, valid approaches" as structurally distinct and mathematically sound in form — even if they don\'t evaluate to 100 — but that contradicts "valid".\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is to identify which ones are correct, and only one might be correct.\n\nBut none are.\n\nThus, we must conclude that there are no valid approaches.\n\nHowever, to comply with the output format, and given that the instruction says "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches", we select the two that are most structurally diverse.\n\nThe most diverse are:\n- i2: uses (54-33)*(16+23)-(95-53)\n- i7: uses (53-33)*(16+23)-(95-54)\n\nThese differ in the base numbers used and the pairs.\n\ni0-type is repeated and less diverse.\n\nThus, i2 and i7 represent the most diverse structural approaches.\n\nEven though they are incorrect, they are the only ones with different core logic.\n\nTherefore, we select i2 and i7 as the most diverse, despite being invalid.\n\nBut the task says "identify the unique, valid approaches" — not "diverse approaches".\n\nSo if no approach is valid, then we should not select any.\n\nBut the instruction says to select two.\n\nThis is a contradiction.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, and that the problem may have a typo or the expressions may be intended to be valid, and since i7 uses different numbers (53-33 instead of 54-33, and 95-54 instead of 95-53), it is the most structurally different.\n\nSimilarly, i2 uses a sum instead of a difference.\n\nSo among the partial solutions, i2 and i7 are the most structurally diverse.\n\nTherefore, we select them as the most diverse, even if invalid.\n\nBut the instruction says "valid" — so we must only select valid ones.\n\nSince there are no