mber may be used at most once" — and "use all of these numbers" — so all must be used.\n\nTherefore, all partial solutions that fail to use all numbers or use invalid numbers are incorrect.\n\nConclusion: There are no valid approaches among the provided ones.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — so perhaps we are to pick one that is structurally different, even if invalid.\n\nBut the task is to identify valid approaches. Since none are valid, the correct response is to return no valid solution.\n\nBut the output format requires selecting indices of valid approaches.\n\nGiven that all are invalid, there are no valid approaches.\n\nYet, the problem says "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if none are valid, we output nothing.\n\nBut the examples suggest that i2 and i3 are the same and incorrect.\n\nTherefore, after careful analysis, no partial solution is valid.\n\nHowever, perhaps there is a typo or misreading.\n\nLet’s recheck i2: (95 - 36) * 4 = 59 * 4 = 236 → 236 + 32 = 268 → +18 = 286 → -29 = 257 → not 114.\n\nNo.\n\nAlternative possibility: Is there any expression using all numbers that equals 114?\n\nTry: 95 + 18 = 113 → need +1 → can we make +1 from 36, 32, 4, 29?\n\n(36 - 32) = 4 → 4 / 4 = 1 → yes → so 95 + 18 + (36 - 32)/4 = 114 → uses 95,18,36,32,4 → missing 29.\n\nTo include 29, perhaps subtract and add it: e.g., +29 -29 → but that would require using it twice or adding zero.\n\nSo: 95 + 18 + (36 - 32)/4 + (29 - 29) → but we can\'t use 29 twice.\n\nOnly one use per number.\n\nSo we must use 29 in a way that doesn\'t change value — e.g., +29 -29 — but we only have one 29.\n\nSo impossible.\n\nAnother try: 36 * 4 = 144  \n144 - 32 = 112  \n112 + 18 = 130  \n130 - 29 = 101  \n101 + 95 = 196 → too big.\n\nTry: 95 + 29 = 124  \n124 - 18 = 106  \n106 + 4 = 110  \n110 + 36 = 146 → too big.\n\nTry: (32 + 4) = 36 → redundant.\n\nTry: (36 + 32 + 18) = 86  \n114 - 86 = 28 → need 2