s one of them is valid — but our calculation shows it is not.\n\nAlternatively, did we miscalculate?\n\nRecheck the expression in the examples:\n\n100 + 98 - 49 + 35 + 71 - 89\n\nStep-by-step:\n100 + 98 = 198  \n198 - 49 = 149  \n149 + 35 = 184  \n184 + 71 = 255  \n255 - 89 = 166 → not 194.\n\nSo it is clearly wrong.\n\nTherefore, all partial solutions are incorrect.\n\nThus, there are **no valid approaches** among the given ones.\n\nBut the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — which implies at least one is valid.\n\nThis is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, either:\n- The problem has a typo, or\n- One of the expressions is valid, or\n- We are missing a different operation (like multiplication or division).\n\nBut the partial solutions only use + and -.\n\nThe problem does not restrict operations — it says "arithmetic expression", so multiplication or division could be used.\n\nBut in all provided solutions, only + and - are used.\n\nTherefore, unless a valid expression with multiplication/division exists, and is among the partial solutions, we must reject all.\n\nBut none of the given expressions use multiplication or division.\n\nTherefore, all are invalid.\n\nFinal conclusion: all provided partial solutions are incorrect.\n\nThus, after filtering out incorrect approaches, **no valid approach remains**.\n\nBut since the output format requires indices, and the instruction says to select one, and given that all are identical and incorrect, we must output nothing — but that is not allowed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the task is to recognize that the expression is wrong and thus reject it, and since no valid one exists, we output no indices.\n\nBut the example output has indices.\n\nGiven the contradiction, and since the only expression provided is invalid, and all are duplicates, we must conclude that there is no valid approach in the set.\n\nTherefore, no indices should be selected.\n\nBut to follow the format, and since the instruction says "