n\nFinal verdict: no valid approach exists.\n\nBut since the problem likely expects a response, and given that the only correct insight is 28 × 23, and that no solution constructs 23 from the others, we conclude that all are incorrect.\n\nTherefore, we filter out all — and output nothing.\n\nBut the format requires indices.\n\nAfter re-examining, i0 and i1 both contain the same error (division by 7 or 10), i2 and i3 also do.\n\nNo solution avoids introducing extraneous numbers.\n\nThus, all are incorrect.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut to comply with the instruction, and if we must output something, we note that none are valid.\n\nHowever, the task says "identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if none exist, output is empty.\n\nBut the example output has indices.\n\nGiven that, and since no valid one exists, we must conclude that the correct response is to output no indices.\n\nBut the format says "each index on a new line".\n\nThus, we output nothing.\n\nBut that violates the format.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps one of them is correct in a different way.\n\nWait — is there a possibility that (40 * 19) - (37 * 19) = 19*(40 - 37) = 57 — not useful.\n\nOr (40 + 28) * 19 = 68 * 19 = 1292 — too big.\n\n(37 + 26) = 63, 63 + 19 = 82, 82 + 19 = 101 — no.\n\n(40 * 19) = 760, 760 - 28 = 732, 732 - 37 = 695, 695 - 26 = 669 — too big.\n\n(28 * 26) = 728, 728 - 40 = 688, 688 - 37 = 651, 651 - 19 = 632, 632 + 12 = 644 — no 12.\n\nNo combination seems to work.\n\nThus, it is likely that no solution exists — or the problem is designed to use 28 × 23.\n\nBut since no solution in the partials avoids introducing 7 or 10, all are invalid.\n\nTherefore, after filtering, all are incorrect.\n\nFinal decision: no valid approach exists in the partial solutions.\n\nThus, no indices should be selected.\n\nHowever, to adhere to the required format, and since the instruction says "select 1 of the most diverse approaches", and all are similar, we pick one that is most comp