equires indices on new lines.\n\nThus:\n\n<reasoning>\nAll partial solutions attempt to form 644 by recognizing that 28 × 23 = 644 and try to generate 23 from the remaining numbers. However, each solution introduces auxiliary numbers (such as 7 or 10) not present in the original set (19, 19, 37, 26, 40, 28). This violates the rule of using only the given numbers. For example, (19 + 19) * (37 - 26) + 40 * 28 / 10 introduces 10, which is not among the provided numbers. Similarly, division by 7 is not allowed. None of the solutions construct 23 from the given numbers without introducing extraneous values. Therefore, all approaches are invalid due to violating the constraint of using only the provided numbers. Despite superficial differences in phrasing or structure, all share the same fundamental flaw. Thus, there are no valid approaches among the given partial solutions.\n</reasoning>\n<results>\n\n</results>\n', '<reasoning>\nThe task is to form an expression using exactly the numbers 99, 4, 34, 18, 58, 97 — each used once — with arithmetic operations to evaluate to 126, without including 126 in the expression.\n\nWe analyze each partial solution:\n\n- i0: The proposed expression includes "126" explicitly and uses "(99 + 58 + 18) / (97 / (4 + 34)) * 0 + 126". This is invalid because it includes the target number 126 in the expression, which violates the rule of not using the target number. Additionally, multiplying by zero makes the entire expression evaluate to 126, which is a trivial and incorrect approach — it relies on a zero term and directly includes the target. Therefore, i0 is invalid and does not meet the problem constraints.\n\n- i1, i2, i3: All three present the same expression: "99 + 58 - 97 + 34 / 18 * 4". This is a superficially identical expression across all three. We must evaluate whether this expression actually evaluates to 126.\n\nLet’s compute it step by step with standard order of operations (PEMDAS):\n\nFirst, division and multiplication from left to right:\n34 / 18 = 1.888... (approximately)\nThen, 1.888... * 4 ≈ 7.555...\n\nNow compute the rest:\n99 + 58 = 157  \n157 - 97 = 60  \n60 + 7.555... ≈ 67.555...\n\nThis is far from 126. So the expression does not evaluate to 126. Therefore, it is incorrect.\n\nEven though the expressions are identical, they are mathematically invalid. They do not produce the target value.\n\nThus, none of the provided partial solutions correctly evaluates to 126 using the given numbers and operations.\n\nHowever, the instruction says: "Identify the unique, valid approaches" and "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — implying that among the given, one may be valid or correct.\n\nBut in this case, all solutions either:\n- Include the target number (i0), or\n- Are mathematically incorrect (i1, i2, i3)\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches among the given partial solutions.\n\nBut the instruction says to "filter out incorrect approaches and identify the unique, valid approaches" — so if no valid one exists, then we must conclude that none are valid.\n\nHowever, the instruction also says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — suggesting that at least one is valid.\n\nBut upon close inspection, all expressions are either invalid or trivially incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the only possible conclusion is that none of the provided partial solutions are valid.\n\nBut since the problem requires selecting one valid approach, and all are invalid, we must consider whether any of them might be misinterpreted.\n\nNote: i0 is explicitly flagged as including 126 — which is against the rules. So it is invalid.\n\ni1, i2, i3: same expression, wrong value.\n\nThus, no valid approach exists in the list.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — which implies that among the given, one is valid and the rest are not.\n\nBut none are valid.\n\nTherefore, we must conclude that the provided partial solutions contain no valid expressions.\n\nBut since th