lculation. Invalid.\n\ni2: Proposes 75 * 8 + 38 + 24 + 22 + 8 = 692.  \nCheck: 75*8 = 600; 600 + 38 = 638; +24 = 662; +22 = 684; +8 = 692 → correct.  \nAll numbers used: 75, 38, 24, 22, 8, 8 → yes.  \nValid expression.\n\ni3: Proposes 75 * 8 + 22 + 38 + 24 + 8 = 692.  \nSame as i2 — just reordered. Addition is commutative. Same numbers, same operations. Identical in structure and correctness. Duplicate of i2.\n\ni4: Same as i2 and i3 — 75 * 8 + 22 + 38 + 24 + 8 → same expression, just reordered. Duplicate.\n\ni5: 75 * 8 + 38 * 8 + 24 + 22  \nCompute: 75*8 = 600; 38*8 = 304; 600 + 304 = 904; +24 = 928; +22 = 950 → too high. Incorrect. Uses multiplication of 38 and 8, which is not needed and exceeds target. Invalid.\n\ni6: 75 * 8 + 38 + 24 + 8 + 22 → same as i2 and i3. Correct and valid. Repeats the same logic.\n\ni7: Same as i5 — 75 * 8 + 38 * 8 + 24 + 22 → evaluates to 950 → incorrect. Invalid.\n\nThus, the only valid expressions are those that sum to 692 via 75 * 8 = 600, and then add the remaining numbers (38, 24, 22, and one 8) to make 92.\n\nAll valid expressions are permutations of:  \n75 * 8 + 38 + 24 + 22 + 8\n\nThis is one unique core approach: use multiplication of 75 and 8 to get 600, then sum the rest to get 92.\n\nThe other approaches (i0, i1, i3, i4, i6) are just reorders or duplicates of this valid method.\n\ni5 and i7 use multiplication of 38 and 8, which is invalid because it leads to a much larger value and does not yield 692.\n\nTherefore, despite different phrasing or ordering, all valid solutions are variations of the same correct method.\n\nWe are to select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches.\n\nBut since all valid ones are structurally identical, diversity is minimal.\n\nHowever, we must pick two distinct *indices* that represent valid, correct, and distinct in form (though not in logic).\n\ni2 and i4 are both valid and correct, but i4 presents the expression with 22 first, which is a different order. Similarly, i6 ends with +8 and includes 22 earlier.\n\nBut the expression is mathematically identical in structure — all are just permutations of the same sum.\n\nThus, no true diversity in approach.\n\nBut the instruction says: "Select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches."\n\nGiven that all valid ones are the same in logic, and only one unique method exists, we must pick two that represent valid and correctly structured expressions.\n\nAmong them, i2 and i4 are both correct and present the same method in different orders — still, they are not diverse.\n\nHowever, i6 presents the same expression with a different grouping: 75 * 8 + 38 + 24 + 8 + 22 — same numbers, same operations.\n\nBut in i2, the expression is: 75 * 8 + 38 + 24 + 22 + 8 — ends with +8.\n\nIn i6, it ends with +22.\n\nStill, no structural difference.\n\nTherefore, there is no true diversity in approach.\n\nBut since the problem says "select 2.0 of the most diverse approaches", and only one valid method exists, we must pick two that are valid and distinct in form (even if logically equivalent).\n\nThus, we select i2 and i4 — both correctly evaluate to 692 and use the same valid method, but differ in order of addition — a minor variation.\n\ni5 and i7 are invalid due to incorrect multiplication.\n\nAll others are duplicates or invalid.\n\nThus, the only valid and correct approaches are i2, i3, i4, i6 — all equivalent.\n\nWe pick two that are distinct in order but same in logic.\n\nFinal selection: i2 and i4.\n\nNote: i3 is also valid, but i3 is listed in the same block as i4 — and i3\'s content is identical to i4.\n\nLooking at the data:\n\n- i2: "75 * 8 + 38 + 24 + 22 + 8"\n- i3: "75 * 8 + 22 + 38 + 24 + 8"\n- i4: "75 * 8 + 22 + 38 + 24 + 8"\n- i6: "75 * 8 + 38 + 24 + 8 + 22"\n\nSo i3 and i4 are identical. i2 and i6 are different orders.\n\nThus, the most diverse valid expressions are i2 and i6 — one with 22 at the end, one with 22 in the middle.\n\nThey differ in order, so they are slightly more diverse.\n\nTherefore, we select i2 and i6.\n\nB